 may be redundant with other aspect ratio measures, like PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO, and has a narrow range, limiting its discriminatory power.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Drop feature: SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# Reason: This feature consistently has values close to zero in the dataset (e.g., mean = 6.38, std = 4.89), which makes it unlikely to contribute significant discriminatory power.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Feature Engineering Steps:\n\n#### Step 1: Add interaction features between related attributes\nInteraction terms between related attributes can capture complex relationships within the dataset that are not apparent from individual features alone. These terms might be particularly useful in classification tasks where certain combinations of features are indicative of specific vehicle types.\n\npython\n# INTERACTION_COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY\n# Multiply COMPACTNESS and CIRCULARITY to capture joint geometric properties.\n# Usefulness: Vehicles may have unique combinations of compactness and circularity due to their shape and viewing angle.\ndf['INTERACTION_COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] * df['CIRCULARITY']\n\n# INTERACTION_RADIUS_RATIO_SCATTER_RATIO\n# Multiply RADIUS_RATIO and SCATTER_RATIO to capture radius consistency and 2D inertia distribution.\n# Usefulness: Shape irregularities combined with scatter distribution might differentiate between classes like OPEL and VAN.\ndf['INTERACTION_RADIUS_RATIO_SCATTER_RATIO'] = df['RADIUS_RATIO'] * df['SCATTER_RATIO']\n\n# INTERACTION_ELONGATEDNESS_PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY\n# Multiply ELONGATEDNESS and PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY to capture rectangular properties in elongated objects.\n# Usefulness: Captures how elongatedness interacts with bounding rectangle properties, which could vary between vehicle shapes like BUS or VAN.\ndf['INTERACTION_ELONGATEDNESS_PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY'] = df['ELONGATEDNESS'] * df['PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY']\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2: Transform skewed features to improve their distribution\nSeveral features, such as SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS, have distributions that could be highly skewed. Applying transformations like logarithms or square roots can reduce the skewness, making the data more normalized and improving model performance.\n\npython\n# LOG_SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS\n# Apply log transformation to SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS to reduce skewness.\n# Usefulness: Reducing skewness likely improves interpretability and downstream model predictions for features with extreme ranges.\ndf['LOG_SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'] = df['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'].apply(lambda x: np.log(x + 1))\n\n# SQRT_KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS\n# Apply square root transformation to KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS to reduce skewness.\n# Usefulness: Used on features with non-negative values to gently compress high-value ranges without strongly altering distribution.\ndf['SQRT_KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'] = df['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'].apply(np.sqrt)\n\n# RECIPROCAL_HOLLOWS_RATIO\n# Apply reciprocal transformation to HOLLOWS_RATIO to emphasize smaller-area hollows while compressing higher values.\n# Usefulness: This highlights subtler differences in hollow ratios for certain vehicle classes.\ndf['RECIPROCAL_HOLLOWS_RATIO'] = 1 / (df['HOLLOWS_RATIO'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3: Create ratios and differences to standardize and capture contrasts\nRatio and difference features are useful for revealing proportional and comparative relationships between features that offer unique insights into the geometry.\n\npython\n# RATIO_COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY\n# Create ratio of COMPACTNESS to CIRCULARITY to standardize their relationship.\n# Usefulness: A normalized relationship between compactness and circularity may discriminate between differently shaped vehicles.\ndf['RATIO_COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / (df['CIRCULARITY'] + 1e-6)\n\n# DIFF_RADIUS_RATIO_SCATTER_RATIO\n# Create difference between RADIUS_RATIO and SCATTER_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: Captures the degree to which radius irregularities deviate from inertia distribution, helping to separate categories.\ndf['DIFF_RADIUS_RATIO_SCATTER_RATIO'] = df['RADIUS_RATIO'] - df['SCATTER_RATIO']\n\n# RATIO_MAJOR_MINOR_SCALED_VARIANCE\n# Calculate ratio of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS to SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS.\n# Usefulness: Highlights proportional differences in variance that might correspond to asymmetry in shapes of vehicles.\ndf['RATIO_MAJOR_MINOR_SCALED_VARIANCE'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] / (df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Dropping Columns\nDrop features that are highly correlated or redundant after feature generation to reduce multicollinearity and simplify the model.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# Highly skewed and now represented by transformed feature LOG_SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# Redundant with transformed feature RECIPROCAL_HOLLOWS_RATIO, which emphasizes smaller hollows more effectively.\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# Replaced with SQRT_KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS to better represent data by reducing outliers.\ndf.drop(columns=['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nBy constructing meaningful interaction terms, transforming skewed features, and adding ratios/differences, you enhance the dataset's representation of structural and geometric relationships inherent in vehicles. Dropping redundant columns ensures the model is not unnecessarily complex, and learning is optimized.", "### Feature Engineering Steps:\n\n#### Step 1: Add a new feature that captures shape irregularity\npython\n# FEATURE: IRREGULARITY_INDEX\n# Feature description: The ratio of RADIUS_RATIO to MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY to capture how irregularly shaped the silhouette is.\n# Usefulness: Vehicles with more consistent shapes (e.g., VAN, BUS) will likely have a lower irregularity compared to asymmetrical ones like OPEL or SAAB.\ndf['IRREGULARITY_INDEX'] = df['RADIUS_RATIO'] / (df['MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Combine variance features to capture overall spread\npython\n# FEATURE: TOTAL_SCALED_VARIANCE\n# Feature description: The sum of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS and SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS to quantify total distribution spread.\n# Usefulness: Captures the overall spread of the silhouette, which is likely to differ significantly across vehicle types due to their structural design.\ndf['TOTAL_SCALED_VARIANCE'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] + df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS']\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Normalize compactness and circularity to understand relative proportions\npython\n# FEATURE: NORMALIZED_COMPACTNESS\n# Feature description: The difference between COMPACTNESS and CIRCULARITY, normalized by their sum to express their proportional relationship.\n# Usefulness: Quantifies the balance between compactness and circularity, which may uniquely describe certain vehicles based on their viewing angle.\ndf['NORMALIZED_COMPACTNESS'] = (df['COMPACTNESS'] - df['CIRCULARITY']) / (df['COMPACTNESS'] + df['CIRCULARITY'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Features:\n\n#### Drop redundant or low-variance features:\npython\n# Explanation why the column KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# This feature has a limited range and low variance (std = 8.88), which likely limits its contribution to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# This feature is likely redundant with other features such as RADIUS_RATIO or PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO, which already capture aspect ratio-related information.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS has low mean (6.38) and low standard deviation (4.89), making it unlikely to have significant discriminative power for classification.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nBy introducing insightful features, such as irregularity and variance summaries, and dropping redundant or low-informative features, these steps aim to enhance downstream classification performance while keeping the dataset robust.", "### Feature Engineering Steps:\n\n#### Step 1: Create Combined Shape Features  \nLeverage ratios of existing shape descriptors to capture relationships.\n\npython\n# RATIO_COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY\n# Ratio of COMPACTNESS and CIRCULARITY.\n# Usefulness: Combines two core shape attributes. Compactness relates to perimeter and area, while Circularity includes radius. Their combination can distinguish shapes across vehicle types.\ndf['RATIO_COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / (df['CIRCULARITY'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Normalize Variances  \nCapture relative contributions from major and minor axes' variances.\n\npython\n# VARIANCE_RATIO\n# Ratio of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS to SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS.\n# Usefulness: Captures asymmetry in the shape. Certain vehicles may have more elongated forms, like buses, while others like vans may have less contrast between axes.\ndf['VARIANCE_RATIO'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] / (df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Standardize Scatter and Elongatedness  \nCombine SCATTER_RATIO and ELONGATEDNESS into a ratio to extract additional depth.\n\npython\n# SCATTER_ELONGATEDNESS_RATIO\n# Ratio of SCATTER_RATIO to ELONGATEDNESS.\n# Usefulness: Helps identify how internal mass distribution relates to the elongation of the object. For instance, buses may have high elongatedness but relatively even scatter distribution.\ndf['SCATTER_ELONGATEDNESS_RATIO'] = df['SCATTER_RATIO'] / (df['ELONGATEDNESS'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Irrelevant or Redundant Features\n\n#### Drop column with low variance and little contribution to overall classification.\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO has a narrow range and overlaps conceptually with PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Drop column that is underrepresented in data.\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS has inconsistent and low values, as the mean and standard deviation are small (mean = 6.38, std = 4.89). Reduces complexity without losing information.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Drop redundant feature normalized elsewhere.\npython\n# Explanation why the column KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS provides limited additional value, and more meaningful transformations can represent its impact better.\ndf.drop(columns=['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Conclusion:\n- The new features (ratios and combinations) focus on leveraging existing geometric relationships to gain insights.\n- Dropped features target those with either low variance, redundancy, or limited impact. This ensures the final dataset improves predictive performance for the task at hand (vehicle classification).", "### Feature Engineering Steps:\n\n#### Step 1: Generate ratio to capture internal shape complexity\npython\n# Feature name: COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio between COMPACTNESS and CIRCULARITY to capture the relationship between perimeter-area interactions and radius-area interactions.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines two core shape descriptors, helping differentiate shapes with distinct geometric complexities. For example, vehicles with circular vs. angular geometries (e.g., VAN vs. BUS) may exhibit varying ratios.\ndf['COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / (df['CIRCULARITY'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Create a feature indicating overall rectangularity and spread\npython\n# Feature name: AREA_RECTANGULARITY_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY to MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY to capture how closely a silhouette approximates a rectangle along principal axes.\n# Usefulness: A low ratio could indicate irregular or elongated shapes (e.g., SAAB), and a high ratio could correspond to shapes with tight rectangular bounds (e.g., VAN).\ndf['AREA_RECTANGULARITY_RATIO'] = df['PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY'] / (df['MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Combine variance features to measure shape asymmetry\npython\n# Feature name: SCALED_VARIANCE_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS to SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS to highlight asymmetry in variance distribution.\n# Usefulness: Asymmetry in variance can help identify elongated shapes (e.g., BUS) versus more symmetric ones (e.g., OPEL). This feature emphasizes proportional spread across axes.\ndf['SCALED_VARIANCE_RATIO'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] / (df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns:\n#### Drop columns that add little value or are effectively represented by transformed features.\n\n#### Drop SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# This feature has very low mean and standard deviation (mean = 6.38, std = 4.89), indicating it contributes little discriminatory power between classes.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Drop KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS\npython\n# Explanation why the column KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# This feature has a limited value range and a low variance (std = 8.88), making it less informative. The transformed feature SCALED_VARIANCE_RATIO better captures variance effects.\ndf.drop(columns=['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Drop MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# This feature has a very small range (mean = 8.58, std = 4.22) and overlaps conceptually with other aspect ratio features, such as PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion:\n- The added features focus on ratios that capture essential geometric properties, such as shape complexity, rectangularity, and asymmetry.\n- Dropped features remove redundancies or low-variance features that add little value to classification, ensuring the dataset captures the most meaningful characteristics of vehicle silhouettes.", "python\n# Feature: COMPACTNESS_SQUARED\n# Feature description: Square of the COMPACTNESS value.\n# Usefulness: COMPACTNESS already encodes the relationship between perimeter and area. Squaring it can amplify differences in this relationship, which may be important for very compact or spread-out vehicle types.\ndf['COMPACTNESS_SQUARED'] = df['COMPACTNESS']  2\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: VARIANCE_COMBINED\n# Feature description: Weighted combination of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS and SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS.\n# Usefulness: Helps to capture the overall spread of the silhouette, bringing in contributions from both major and minor axes for vehicle classification.\ndf['VARIANCE_COMBINED'] = 0.5 * (df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] + df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: HOLLOWS_NORMALIZED\n# Feature description: Ratio of HOLLOWS_RATIO to MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY.\n# Usefulness: Normalizing the hollow area by the silhouette's maximum rectangularity can better represent internal structural complexity across different vehicle types.\ndf['HOLLOWS_NORMALIZED'] = df['HOLLOWS_RATIO'] / (df['MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS has low mean (6.38) and very small variance (std = 4.89), indicating it contributes little to distinguishing between vehicle types.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO has a narrow range (mean = 8.58, std = 4.22) and overlaps conceptually with other features like PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO, making it redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS has limited variance (std = 8.88) and often represents outlier behavior. Other transformations like square root would handle this information better, making the original feature less useful.\ndf.drop(columns=['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Feature Engineering Steps:\n\n#### Step 1: Add a feature to capture the disproportionate spread of mass\npython\n# Feature name: VARIANCE_SPREAD_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS to SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS.\n# Usefulness: Highlights whether the silhouette's spread is more concentrated along the minor or major axis, which could reflect differences in vehicle geometry (e.g., elongated shapes like BUS vs. compact shapes like VAN).\ndf['VARIANCE_SPREAD_RATIO'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'] / (df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Capture the holistic relationship between compactness and rectangularity\npython\n# Feature name: RECTANGULAR_COMPACTNESS\n# Feature description: Product of PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY and COMPACTNESS.\n# Usefulness: Combines compactness and rectangularity to represent overall shape tightness and density, useful for identifying irregular shapes or differentiating between compact and elongated vehicles.\ndf['RECTANGULAR_COMPACTNESS'] = df['PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY'] * df['COMPACTNESS']\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Measure symmetry through elongation and scatter\npython\n# Feature name: ELONGATION_SCATTER_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of ELONGATEDNESS to SCATTER_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: Balances elongation against the scatter distribution of the shape. Elongated vehicles (e.g., BUS) may have distinct scatter relative to shorter vehicles (e.g., OPEL or SAAB).\ndf['ELONGATION_SCATTER_RATIO'] = df['ELONGATEDNESS'] / (df['SCATTER_RATIO'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Irrelevant or Low-Value Columns:\n\n#### Drop SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# This feature has consistently low mean (6.38) and variance (std = 4.89), making it unlikely to contribute significant information to the classification.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Drop KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS\npython\n# Explanation why the column KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# This feature has a narrow range (values mostly between 0 and 40) and low variance (std = 8.88). Its importance is likely overshadowed by more informative features like SCALED_VARIANCE_RATIO or RADIUS_RATIO.\ndf.drop(columns=['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Drop MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# This feature has a small range (mean = 8.58, std = 4.22) and overlaps conceptually with PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO, reducing its individual importance in distinguishing vehicle types.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n- Added features focus on relationships between variance, compactness, and elongation to enhance the representation of vehicle geometry.\n- Dropped features either contribute low variance, overlap with other metrics, or provide limited discriminatory power for the task at hand.", "python\n# Feature: COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of COMPACTNESS to CIRCULARITY to capture the relationship between perimeter-area interactions and radius-area interactions.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines two fundamental shape metrics, helping to distinguish different vehicle types based on how compact and circular their silhouettes are.\ndf['COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / (df['CIRCULARITY'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: VARIANCE_ASYMMETRY\n# Feature description: Difference between SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS and SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS.\n# Usefulness: This feature highlights asymmetry in the spread of the silhouette's variance along its axes, which could help differentiate elongated vehicles (e.g., BUS) from more symmetric ones (e.g., OPEL).\ndf['VARIANCE_ASYMMETRY'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] - df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: NORMALIZED_HOLLOWNESS\n# Feature description: Ratio of HOLLOWS_RATIO to PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY to normalize hollow area by the bounding rectangularity.\n# Usefulness: Helps assess the internal structure and irregular patterns within vehicles, especially distinguishing vehicles with more or less hollow complexity.\ndf['NORMALIZED_HOLLOWNESS'] = df['HOLLOWS_RATIO'] / (df['PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS has low mean (6.38) and low variance (std = 4.89), implying it may contribute little to vehicle classification.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS has limited range and a lower variance (std = 8.88), making it less useful for discriminating geometric patterns.\ndf.drop(columns=['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO has a narrow range (mean = 8.58, std = 4.22) and overlaps conceptually with other aspect ratio features like PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO, making it redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend"]###,
